insert into public.flashcards (id, cert, domain, objective, deck, front, back) values

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------- 1.1 (14) --
('n-f-400','netplus',1,'1.1','core',$q$A capture shows one unit of data carrying an Ethernet header, an IP header and a TCP header. What is that unit called at Layers 2, 3 and 4?$q$,$q$Frame at Layer 2, packet at Layer 3, segment at Layer 4. The name always refers to the outermost header that the layer in question is responsible for - the same bytes on the wire are a frame to the switch and a packet to the router.$q$),
('n-f-401','netplus',1,'1.1','acronym',$q$PDU$q$,$q$Protocol Data Unit - the generic name for the unit of data at a given OSI layer (data, segment or datagram, packet, frame, bits). Naming the PDU tells you which layer you are reasoning about.$q$),
('n-f-402','netplus',1,'1.1','core',$q$What belongs to OSI Layer 1, and what symptom points there?$q$,$q$Cabling, connectors, transceivers, patch panels, repeaters, hubs, and the signalling and encoding on the medium. The symptom is no link light, high error or CRC counters, or a port that will not come up at all - Layer 1 faults break everything above them.$q$),
('n-f-403','netplus',1,'1.1','core',$q$What does OSI Layer 2 do, and what are its two sublayers?$q$,$q$Frames data for delivery across one link, using MAC addressing, and detects corrupt frames with the FCS trailer. Its sublayers are LLC (interfaces with the upper layers) and MAC (controls access to the medium). Switches, 802.1Q VLAN tagging and ARP responses live here.$q$),
('n-f-404','netplus',1,'1.1','core',$q$What does OSI Layer 3 add, and which devices and protocols operate there?$q$,$q$Logical IP addressing, routing between different networks, TTL-based loop prevention and fragmentation. Routers and Layer 3 switches forward here; IPv4, IPv6 and ICMP are Layer 3 protocols. If local traffic works but remote traffic does not, start here.$q$),
('n-f-405','netplus',1,'1.1','core',$q$What are the four jobs of OSI Layer 4?$q$,$q$Segmentation of the data stream and reassembly in order, identifying the endpoint application by port number, flow control (TCP windowing), and choosing reliability (TCP) or speed (UDP). A blocked port number is a Layer 4 fault, not a Layer 3 one.$q$),
('n-f-406','netplus',1,'1.1','core',$q$What is OSI Layer 5 responsible for?$q$,$q$Establishing, maintaining, synchronising and tearing down the dialogue between two applications - session setup, session identifiers and orderly termination. Remote procedure calls and session tokens are typical examples; it manages the conversation, not the formatting of the data.$q$),
('n-f-407','netplus',1,'1.1','core',$q$What does OSI Layer 6 do?$q$,$q$Translates data between the application format and a common transmission format: character encoding, file format representation, compression, and encryption or decryption. TLS is conventionally placed here on the exam, and format mismatches (a file that arrives but cannot be read) are the classic Layer 6 symptom.$q$),
('n-f-408','netplus',1,'1.1','core',$q$What actually lives at OSI Layer 7?$q$,$q$The network-facing services that applications call - HTTP and HTTPS, DNS, SMTP, FTP, SNMP, LDAP. Layer 7 is not the application itself: the browser is software on the host, while the HTTP exchange it generates is the Layer 7 protocol.$q$),
('n-f-409','netplus',1,'1.1','core',$q$What is the difference between encapsulation and de-encapsulation?$q$,$q$Encapsulation happens on the way down at the sender: each layer wraps the payload from above in its own header (and at Layer 2 a trailer). De-encapsulation happens on the way up at the receiver: each layer strips its own header, checks it, and hands the remainder to the layer above.$q$),
('n-f-410','netplus',1,'1.1','core',$q$As a packet crosses several routers, which headers change and which stay the same?$q$,$q$The Layer 2 header is rewritten at every hop, because each link has different MAC addresses. The Layer 3 source and destination IP addresses stay the same end to end (unless NAT is applied), while the TTL is decremented by one at each router. Layer 4 port numbers are untouched by routers.$q$),
('n-f-411','netplus',1,'1.1','core',$q$What are the three OSI-based troubleshooting approaches, and when do you use each?$q$,$q$Bottom-up starts at Layer 1 and is best for suspected physical problems. Top-down starts at Layer 7 and suits a single application failing while everything else works. Divide and conquer starts in the middle, usually with a ping at Layer 3, and halves the search space with one test.$q$),
('n-f-412','netplus',1,'1.1','core',$q$How do you tell a Layer 1 fault from a Layer 2 fault?$q$,$q$No link light, dead port or a physically damaged cable is Layer 1. Link up but no usable traffic is Layer 2: wrong VLAN, duplex mismatch, port security shutting the port, a spanning tree block, or a MAC address not being learned. Layer 1 asks whether there is a signal; Layer 2 asks whether the frames are valid and allowed.$q$),
('n-f-413','netplus',1,'1.1','core',$q$Ping to a server succeeds but the application will not connect. Which layer is implicated and why?$q$,$q$Layer 4 or above. A successful ping proves Layers 1 to 3 all the way to the host, so the remaining suspects are a blocked TCP or UDP port, a service that is not listening, or an application-layer fault. Check the port with a port scan or a telnet-to-port test before touching the routing.$q$),

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------- 1.2 (16) --
('n-f-414','netplus',1,'1.2','core',$q$When do you need a router rather than a Layer 3 switch?$q$,$q$Both route between subnets, but a router terminates WAN circuits, supports diverse interface types, performs NAT and richer policy, and generally has more advanced routing features. A Layer 3 switch routes between VLANs inside the campus at wire speed with many copper ports and is the cheaper choice per port for pure inter-VLAN routing.$q$),
('n-f-415','netplus',1,'1.2','core',$q$How do an IDS and an IPS differ in placement and effect?$q$,$q$An IDS sits out of band on a SPAN port or a tap and receives copies of traffic, so it can only alert after the fact but cannot break production if it fails. An IPS sits inline in the forwarding path and can drop or reset matching traffic - the price being that a false positive blocks legitimate traffic and a failure can interrupt the link.$q$),
('n-f-416','netplus',1,'1.2','core',$q$What does a load balancer do beyond spreading traffic?$q$,$q$Distributes sessions across a server pool (round robin, least connections, weighted), removes members that fail health checks, keeps a client pinned to one backend with session persistence, and often terminates TLS on behalf of the servers (SSL offload). It also hides the number and identity of the backend servers.$q$),
('n-f-417','netplus',1,'1.2','core',$q$What is the difference between a forward proxy and a reverse proxy?$q$,$q$A forward proxy sits in front of internal clients heading out: caching, URL and category filtering, user attribution and anonymity. A reverse proxy sits in front of published servers facing inbound clients: it hides the server topology, terminates TLS and can cache or load balance. Same technology, opposite direction of travel.$q$),
('n-f-418','netplus',1,'1.2','core',$q$NAS or SAN - how do you choose?$q$,$q$NAS is file-level storage served over the normal Ethernet LAN with SMB or NFS; simple, cheap, and the NAS owns the file system. SAN is block-level storage over a dedicated fabric (Fibre Channel or iSCSI) that appears to the server as a raw local disk it formats itself; it is what databases and virtualization hosts expect.$q$),
('n-f-419','netplus',1,'1.2','core',$q$What is the difference between an autonomous access point and a controller-based (lightweight) AP?$q$,$q$An autonomous AP holds its own full configuration and is managed individually - fine for a handful of radios. A lightweight AP receives its configuration from a wireless LAN controller, which also handles channel and power assignment, roaming coordination and central monitoring across the whole fleet.$q$),
('n-f-420','netplus',1,'1.2','acronym',$q$CDN$q$,$q$Content Delivery Network - a geographically distributed set of caching servers that serves static content from an edge location near the user, cutting latency and offloading the origin.$q$),
('n-f-421','netplus',1,'1.2','core',$q$What problem does a CDN solve, and what does it not solve?$q$,$q$It solves distance: static assets are cached at edge locations near users, so round-trip latency drops and origin bandwidth is offloaded, and the distributed footprint helps absorb volumetric attacks. It does not accelerate uncacheable, per-user dynamic responses - those still have to be generated at the origin.$q$),
('n-f-422','netplus',1,'1.2','core',$q$What is the difference between a stateless packet filter, a stateful firewall and a next-generation firewall?$q$,$q$A stateless filter (a router ACL) evaluates each packet alone against addresses, protocols and ports. A stateful firewall tracks connections, so the return traffic of an allowed session is permitted automatically. A next-generation firewall adds application awareness, user identity and integrated IPS, so it can distinguish two applications that both use TCP 443.$q$),
('n-f-423','netplus',1,'1.2','core',$q$What does a VPN concentrator do, and how do site-to-site and client-to-site VPNs differ?$q$,$q$A VPN concentrator (headend) terminates many encrypted tunnels at the network edge and applies access policy to them. Site-to-site joins two networks permanently through gateways, so users never install anything. Client-to-site (remote access) puts a client on each endpoint device and connects individual users from anywhere.$q$),
('n-f-424','netplus',1,'1.2','core',$q$What does QoS actually do to fix choppy voice on a congested link?$q$,$q$Classifies traffic, marks it (DSCP EF is the usual marking for voice), and then queues and schedules so marked traffic is serviced first and shaped or policed traffic is held back. QoS only matters where there is congestion - it reallocates a scarce link, it does not create extra bandwidth.$q$),
('n-f-425','netplus',1,'1.2','core',$q$TTL means two different things on the exam - what are they?$q$,$q$In an IP header, TTL is a hop counter that every router decrements and drops at zero, preventing loops and enabling traceroute. In DNS, TTL is the number of seconds a resolver may cache a record - lower it before a planned cutover so clients pick up the new address quickly.$q$),
('n-f-426','netplus',1,'1.2','core',$q$What is the difference between an explicit proxy and a transparent proxy?$q$,$q$An explicit proxy requires the client to be configured (manually or by PAC file) to send requests to it, so an unconfigured client bypasses it entirely. A transparent proxy intercepts traffic in the path with no client configuration, which is harder to evade but can break applications that do certificate pinning.$q$),
('n-f-427','netplus',1,'1.2','core',$q$What problem does session persistence (sticky sessions) solve on a load balancer?$q$,$q$It keeps a given client returning to the same backend server for the life of a session, so server-held state such as a shopping cart or an unshared login session is not lost. Without it, a pure round-robin distribution can land each request on a different server and appear to log the user out at random.$q$),
('n-f-428','netplus',1,'1.2','core',$q$What is the difference between Layer 4 and Layer 7 load balancing?$q$,$q$Layer 4 balances on IP address and port alone, which is fast and protocol-agnostic but blind to content. Layer 7 reads the application data, so it can route by URL path, hostname or cookie, insert headers and terminate TLS - at the cost of more processing per request.$q$),
('n-f-429','netplus',1,'1.2','core',$q$Why prefer another access point over a wireless range extender?$q$,$q$A repeater or extender receives and retransmits on the same radio, roughly halving the throughput for clients behind it and adding latency. An additional AP with its own wired uplink keeps full throughput, and with a controller it also participates in coordinated channel planning and roaming.$q$),

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------- 1.3 (16) --
('n-f-430','netplus',1,'1.3','core',$q$IaaS, PaaS, SaaS - who manages what?$q$,$q$IaaS: provider gives virtual compute, storage and networking; customer owns the guest OS upward. PaaS: provider also owns the OS, runtime and middleware; customer owns only code and data. SaaS: provider runs the whole application; customer owns only its data, users and configuration. The line moves up the stack as you move from IaaS to SaaS.$q$),
('n-f-431','netplus',1,'1.3','core',$q$What are the four cloud deployment models?$q$,$q$Public: multitenant infrastructure shared on a provider platform. Private: dedicated to one organisation, hosted internally or by a third party. Community: shared by several organisations with common compliance needs. Hybrid: private and public linked so workloads and data can move, which is what enables cloud bursting.$q$),
('n-f-432','netplus',1,'1.3','acronym',$q$NFV$q$,$q$Network Functions Virtualization - delivering routing, firewall, load balancing and similar network functions as software instances on general-purpose compute rather than as dedicated hardware appliances.$q$),
('n-f-433','netplus',1,'1.3','acronym',$q$VPC$q$,$q$Virtual Private Cloud - a logically isolated network inside a provider cloud where the customer defines its own IP address ranges, subnets, route tables and gateways.$q$),
('n-f-434','netplus',1,'1.3','core',$q$What is the exact difference between scalability and elasticity?$q$,$q$Scalability is the capacity to grow to meet a larger load, typically a planned change. Elasticity is automatic expansion and contraction tracking demand in real time, in both directions, so cost follows usage. Every elastic system is scalable, but a system that can only be grown by hand is not elastic.$q$),
('n-f-435','netplus',1,'1.3','core',$q$Horizontal or vertical scaling - what is the trade-off?$q$,$q$Vertical (scaling up) means giving one instance more CPU or memory: simple, no application change, but bounded by the largest instance available and usually requires downtime. Horizontal (scaling out) means adding more instances behind a load balancer: effectively unbounded and tolerant of node failure, but the application must be able to run stateless or share state.$q$),
('n-f-436','netplus',1,'1.3','core',$q$How does a network security group differ from a network access list in the cloud?$q$,$q$A security group is stateful and attaches to instance interfaces, so return traffic for an allowed session is permitted automatically and rules are usually allow-only. A network access list is stateless and evaluated at the subnet boundary, supports both allow and deny entries in order, and requires an explicit rule for each direction.$q$),
('n-f-437','netplus',1,'1.3','core',$q$What are the common cloud gateway types and what does each do?$q$,$q$Internet gateway: gives a subnet a bidirectional path to the internet. NAT gateway: lets private instances reach out without being reachable inbound. VPN gateway: terminates encrypted tunnels from on-premises sites. Storage gateway: presents cloud storage to on-premises systems using familiar file or block protocols.$q$),
('n-f-438','netplus',1,'1.3','core',$q$Site-to-site VPN or a direct connection to the cloud - how do you choose?$q$,$q$A VPN rides the existing internet circuit: fast to deploy, cheap, encrypted, but latency and throughput vary with internet conditions. A direct connection is a provisioned private circuit: consistent latency, committed bandwidth and no internet exposure, but it costs more and takes weeks to install. Many designs keep a VPN as the backup for the direct connection.$q$),
('n-f-439','netplus',1,'1.3','core',$q$What is multitenancy and what risk does it introduce?$q$,$q$Multiple customers share the same underlying physical infrastructure, with logical isolation keeping their data and traffic apart - it is what makes public cloud economics work. The risk is that a flaw in the isolation, or a noisy neighbour consuming shared resources, can affect a tenant who did nothing wrong.$q$),
('n-f-440','netplus',1,'1.3','core',$q$What does the shared responsibility model always leave with the customer?$q$,$q$Regardless of service model: the data itself, identity and access management, and the customer configuration of the service. The provider is always responsible for the physical facility and the underlying platform. The boundary between them moves up the stack from IaaS to SaaS, but it never reaches the customer data.$q$),
('n-f-441','netplus',1,'1.3','core',$q$What is the difference between a cloud region and an availability zone?$q$,$q$A region is a geographic area, chosen for latency to users and for data residency requirements. An availability zone is one or more discrete data centres within a region with independent power and cooling. Spreading instances across zones survives a single data centre failure; spreading across regions survives a regional event but adds latency and cost.$q$),
('n-f-442','netplus',1,'1.3','core',$q$How can two VPCs communicate privately, and what breaks it?$q$,$q$By peering them directly, or by connecting both to a central transit hub when many networks are involved. The traffic stays on the provider backbone rather than the internet. Overlapping CIDR ranges break it outright - address planning has to be done before the VPCs are built, not after.$q$),
('n-f-443','netplus',1,'1.3','core',$q$How do SDN and NFV differ?$q$,$q$SDN separates the control plane from the data plane and centralises forwarding decisions in a controller - it changes how the network is controlled. NFV replaces dedicated appliances with software instances of the same functions - it changes what the network device is. They are complementary and often deployed together, but neither implies the other.$q$),
('n-f-444','netplus',1,'1.3','core',$q$What makes a cloud subnet public rather than private?$q$,$q$Its route table. A public subnet has a default route pointing at an internet gateway, so resources with a public address are reachable from and can reach the internet. A private subnet has no such route; if its instances need outbound access they get a default route to a NAT gateway instead, which permits outbound only.$q$),
('n-f-445','netplus',1,'1.3','core',$q$What are the five essential characteristics of cloud computing?$q$,$q$On-demand self-service, broad network access, resource pooling, rapid elasticity, and measured service (metered, pay-per-use billing). If an offering is missing one - for example a hosted server you must phone someone to resize - it is hosting rather than true cloud.$q$),

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------- 1.4 (14) --
('n-f-446','netplus',1,'1.4','core',$q$FTP, TFTP, SFTP, FTPS - ports and differences?$q$,$q$FTP: TCP 21 control and TCP 20 data, cleartext. TFTP: UDP 69, no authentication, used by network devices for config and firmware. SFTP: file transfer inside an SSH session on TCP 22. FTPS: FTP wrapped in TLS. SFTP and FTPS both encrypt but are different protocols, not different names for one thing.$q$),
('n-f-447','netplus',1,'1.4','core',$q$What rides on TCP 22, and why did it replace Telnet on TCP 23?$q$,$q$SSH carries encrypted remote shell, and SFTP and SCP file transfer, all on TCP 22. Telnet on TCP 23 sends the session - including the credentials - in cleartext, so anyone capturing the traffic reads the password. Any device still offering Telnet on a management interface is an audit finding.$q$),
('n-f-448','netplus',1,'1.4','acronym',$q$SIP$q$,$q$Session Initiation Protocol - sets up, modifies and tears down voice and video calls on TCP or UDP 5060 in the clear, or 5061 with TLS. It carries only the signalling; RTP carries the actual media stream.$q$),
('n-f-449','netplus',1,'1.4','core',$q$Which ports do the mail protocols use?$q$,$q$SMTP TCP 25 for server-to-server relay, TCP 587 for authenticated client submission and TCP 465 for implicit TLS submission. POP3 TCP 110, or 995 with TLS. IMAP TCP 143, or 993 with TLS. POP3 typically downloads and removes; IMAP keeps mail on the server and syncs across devices.$q$),
('n-f-450','netplus',1,'1.4','core',$q$DNS uses port 53 - but over which transport?$q$,$q$UDP 53 for ordinary queries and responses, because a single small exchange does not justify a handshake. TCP 53 for zone transfers and for responses too large to fit reliably in one datagram. A firewall rule permitting only UDP 53 lets lookups work while silently breaking zone replication.$q$),
('n-f-451','netplus',1,'1.4','core',$q$Which ports does DHCP use, for IPv4 and for IPv6?$q$,$q$IPv4 DHCP uses UDP 67 on the server and UDP 68 on the client. DHCPv6 uses UDP 547 on the server and UDP 546 on the client. It has to be UDP and broadcast for the first exchange, because the client has no address yet - which is also why a relay agent is needed when the server is on another subnet.$q$),
('n-f-452','netplus',1,'1.4','core',$q$HTTP 80 and HTTPS 443 - what exactly does the difference buy?$q$,$q$HTTPS is HTTP carried inside a TLS session on TCP 443, adding confidentiality, integrity and server authentication through the certificate. HTTP on TCP 80 provides none of those, so credentials, cookies and page content are readable and modifiable by anything on the path.$q$),
('n-f-453','netplus',1,'1.4','core',$q$Which port does NTP use, and why does accurate time matter so much?$q$,$q$UDP 123. Without synchronised clocks, log correlation across devices becomes unreliable, Kerberos authentication fails once the skew exceeds its tolerance (five minutes by default), and certificate validity checks can wrongly succeed or fail. Time is a dependency for security, not a convenience.$q$),
('n-f-454','netplus',1,'1.4','core',$q$What are the SNMP ports, and what changed in SNMPv3?$q$,$q$UDP 161 is where the agent listens for the manager to poll it; UDP 162 is where the manager receives traps and informs sent by agents. SNMPv1 and v2c authenticate with a cleartext community string; v3 adds per-user authentication, message integrity and encryption, and is the only version that should be used on production networks.$q$),
('n-f-455','netplus',1,'1.4','core',$q$What are LDAP and LDAPS used for, and on which ports?$q$,$q$LDAP on TCP 389 queries and modifies a directory service - looking up users, groups and their attributes for authentication and authorisation. LDAPS on TCP 636 is the same thing over TLS. Directory binds carry credentials, so plain 389 without StartTLS exposes them on the wire.$q$),
('n-f-456','netplus',1,'1.4','core',$q$Which ports serve SMB, RDP, syslog and the common databases?$q$,$q$SMB TCP 445 for Windows file and printer sharing. RDP TCP 3389 for remote desktop. Syslog UDP 514 to a collector. Microsoft SQL Server TCP 1433 and MySQL TCP 3306. None of 445, 3389, 1433 or 3306 belongs open to the internet - each is a routine target for scanning and brute force.$q$),
('n-f-457','netplus',1,'1.4','core',$q$Unicast, multicast, broadcast, anycast - one line each?$q$,$q$Unicast is one to one. Multicast is one to a subscribed group (IPv4 224.0.0.0/4) and can be routed where configured. Broadcast is one to every host in the broadcast domain (255.255.255.255) and is stopped by routers. Anycast is one to nearest: many hosts share one address and routing picks the closest.$q$),
('n-f-458','netplus',1,'1.4','core',$q$Which IP protocol numbers should you recognise?$q$,$q$ICMP is 1, TCP is 6, UDP is 17, GRE is 47, ESP is 50 and AH is 51. These sit in the protocol field of the IP header, not in a port field - which is why an ACL permitting only TCP and UDP silently blocks a GRE tunnel or an ESP-protected IPSec session.$q$),
('n-f-459','netplus',1,'1.4','acronym',$q$GRE$q$,$q$Generic Routing Encapsulation - IP protocol 47, a tunnelling protocol that encapsulates almost any payload, including multicast and routing protocol traffic, but provides no encryption and so is often paired with IPSec.$q$);
